{
 "module_index":1,
 "module_name": "Expression Matrix Preprocessing",
 "xlsx_file": "1_expression_matrix_preprocessing.xlsx",
 "output_dir": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/analysis\\1_expression_matrix_preprocessing",
 "sheets": [
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/1_expression_matrix_preprocessing\\preprocessed_expression.csv",
 "sheet_name": "Final_Expression",
 "annotation": "Final preprocessed metabolomics expression matrix (2,059 metabolites x18 samples from CD, FE, and NC groups). The first column 'rownames(expr_scaled)' lists metabolite names (e.g. Tandutinib, Octadecanoic acid); columns 'Fc16YH_CD1'~'Fc16YH_CD6' represent the CDI infection group (Clostridioides difficile infection), 'Fc16YH_FE1'~'Fc16YH_FE6' the high-iron diet pre-treatment group, and 'Fc16YH_NC1'~'Fc16YH_NC6' the healthy standard-diet control group. Values are relative abundances after complete preprocessing: half-minimum missing value imputation, column-sum normalization to ppm (×10⁶), log2(x+1) transformation, and row-wise median scaling. All2,059 metabolite medians are exactly1.0, with values ranging ~0.217-2.365, eliminating absolute abundance differences to enable cross-metabolite comparisons. This matrix is the standardised input for downstream PCA (Module2), LIMMA differential analysis (Module4), WGCNA co-expression network analysis (Module6), and CMeans clustering (Module7), designed to reveal metabolic changes in bile acids, SCFAs, siderophores, and haem-related metabolites underlying high-iron diet modulation of CDI susceptibility."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/1_expression_matrix_preprocessing\\preprocess_step2_normalized.csv",
 "sheet_name": "Normalized_PPM",
 "annotation": "Intermediate expression matrix after Step2: column-sum normalisation (relative abundance in ppm). The first column 'rownames(expr_normalized)' contains metabolite identifiers; the18 sample columns correspond to three experimental groups: CD (Fc16YH_CD1-6, C. difficile infection), FE (Fc16YH_FE1-6, high-iron diet pre-treatment before infection), and NC (Fc16YH_NC1-6, healthy control). Each value represents the metabolite's ion count divided by its sample total and multiplied by1×10⁶. After normalisation, all sample column sums converge to ~999,610-999,729 ppm (SD=37.89), effectively removing systematic technical variation from sample preparation and instrument sensitivity. Values range from ~2.59 to ~84,110 ppm. High-iron diet and CDI are known to alter bile acid profiles (taurocholate, chenodeoxycholate), SCFAs, and iron-related metabolites (haem, siderophores, lipocalin-2 targets); this normalised matrix preserves these biological signals for subsequent log transformation and scaling."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/1_expression_matrix_preprocessing\\preprocess_step3_log2.csv",
 "sheet_name": "Log2_Transformed",
 "annotation": "Intermediate expression matrix after Step3: log2(x+1) transformation applied to the ppm-normalised data. The first column 'rownames(expr_log)' lists metabolite names; the18 sample columns represent the CD (Fc16YH_CD1-6), FE (Fc16YH_FE1-6), and NC (Fc16YH_NC1-6) groups. Values now range from ~1.85 to ~16.36 (mean ~8.45), compressing the wide dynamic range of raw metabolomics data and shifting the distribution from heavily right-skewed to approximately normal. This transformation stabilises variance across low- and high-abundance metabolites and meets normality assumptions required for parametric tests such as LIMMA differential analysis, which will compare CD vs NC to identify CDI-induced metabolic perturbations (e.g. bile acid dysregulation, SCFA reduction, siderophore elevation) and FE vs CD to assess the modulatory effect of high-iron diet pre-treatment on the infection metabolome."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/1_expression_matrix_preprocessing\\preprocess_step3_scaled.csv",
 "sheet_name": "Median_Scaled",
 "annotation": "Intermediate expression matrix after Step4: row-wise median scaling applied to the log2(x+1)-transformed data. The first column 'rownames(expr_scaled)' contains metabolite names; the18 sample columns correspond to CD (Fc16YH_CD1-6), FE (Fc16YH_FE1-6), and NC (Fc16YH_NC1-6) groups. Each value is the log2-transformed abundance divided by the median of that metabolite across all18 samples, resulting in all2,059 metabolites having a median of exactly1.0. Values span ~0.217-2.365 (mean=1.025, SD=0.127). Group means after scaling are CD=1.037, FE=1.005, NC=1.033—all centred near1.0 while preserving relative fold differences. This scaling eliminates absolute abundance disparities between high- and low-abundance metabolites, enabling fair cross-metabolite comparisons in heatmap visualisation and clustering. This file is identical to the final 'preprocessed_expression' output."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/1_expression_matrix_preprocessing\\sampleinfo.csv",
 "sheet_name": "Sample_Info",
 "annotation": "Sample metadata table for the metabolomics study (21 entries). 'ID': unique sample identifier; 'sample_name': abbreviated sample label; 'sample_info': experimental group assignment — 'QC' (Fc16YH_QC01-03, quality control samples for technical stability assessment, not present in the expression matrix), 'Clostridium difficile infection' (Fc16YH_CD1-6, CDI group, n=6), 'high iron diet before' (Fc16YH_FE1-6, high-iron diet pre-treatment group, n=6), and 'Standard (control)' (Fc16YH_NC1-6, healthy control group, n=6). The three-group design enables key comparisons: CD vs NC reveals CDI-induced metabolic dysbiosis (bile acid dysregulation, SCFA decline, siderophore elevation), FE vs CD assesses how high-iron diet modulates infection metabolome (iron homeostasis, haem, ferritin, bile acid metabolism), and FE vs NC evaluates the effect of high-iron diet alone on normal gut metabolism."
 }
 ]
}

{
 "module_index":5,
 "module_name": "KEGG Functional Analysis",
 "xlsx_file": "5_kegg_functional_analysis.xlsx",
 "output_dir": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/analysis\\5_kegg_functional_analysis",
 "sheets": [
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/5_kegg_functional_analysis\\tables\\kegg_enrichment_CD_vs_NC.csv",
 "sheet_name": "kegg_enrich_CD_vs_NC",
 "annotation": "Fisher's exact test results for KEGG pathway enrichment analysis comparing CD (C. difficile infection) vs NC (normal control) groups. Columns: Pathway (KEGG pathway name and ID), Class (KEGG pathway class hierarchy, e.g., '09100 Metabolism'), Count_in_Sig (number of significantly changed metabolites mapped to this pathway), Count_in_BG (number of background metabolites mapped to this pathway), Sig_Size (total number of significant differential metabolites tested), Bg_Size (total number of background metabolites), pvalue (raw Fisher's exact test p-value), pvalue_adj (FDR-corrected p-value), enriched (TRUE/FALSE indicator). Top enriched pathways include 'Folding, sorting and degradation' (3/4, p=0.042) and 'Endocrine system' (4/8, p=0.092), reflecting CDI-induced host stress responses and endocrine changes. Note: no pathway reached FDR <0.05 significance due to limited KEGG metabolite coverage (21.7%) and conservative multiple testing correction."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/5_kegg_functional_analysis\\tables\\kegg_enrichment_FE_vs_CD.csv",
 "sheet_name": "kegg_enrich_FE_vs_CD",
 "annotation": "Fisher's exact test results for KEGG pathway enrichment analysis comparing FE (high-iron diet + CDI) vs CD (standard diet + CDI) groups. This is the core comparison assessing the impact of high-iron diet on CDI host metabolism. Columns: Pathway, Class, Count_in_Sig, Count_in_BG, Sig_Size (146 differential metabolites), Bg_Size (447 background metabolites), pvalue, pvalue_adj, enriched. Top enriched pathways: 'Biosynthesis of other secondary metabolites' (36/87, p=0.037) and 'Cell growth and death' (4/5, p=0.041), indicating high-iron diet influences microbial secondary metabolism and host cell processes. 'Lipid metabolism' (27/73, p=0.233) shows enrichment trend aligning with expected reduction of SCFA-producing beneficial bacteria under high-iron conditions (kb: pharmaceutics-18-00651). However, no pathway passed FDR correction due to limited annotation coverage and conservative Fisher test."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/5_kegg_functional_analysis\\tables\\kegg_enrichment_FE_vs_NC.csv",
 "sheet_name": "kegg_enrich_FE_vs_NC",
 "annotation": "Fisher's exact test results for KEGG pathway enrichment analysis comparing FE (high-iron diet + CDI) vs NC (normal control) groups, representing the overall effect of high-iron diet on the CDI metabolic landscape. Columns: Pathway, Class, Count_in_Sig (136 significant metabolites), Count_in_BG, Sig_Size, Bg_Size (447), pvalue, pvalue_adj, enriched. Top pathways include 'Cell growth and death' (3/5, p=0.168) and 'Lipid metabolism' (26/73, p=0.180), with 'Biosynthesis of other secondary metabolites' (29/87, p=0.297) also showing enrichment. The pattern is consistent with FE_vs_CD but attenuated, reflecting the combined dietary and infection effects. No pathways met FDR significance thresholds."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/5_kegg_functional_analysis\\tables\\kegg_gsva_differential_CD_vs_NC.csv",
 "sheet_name": "gsva_diff_CD_vs_NC",
 "annotation": "LIMMA differential analysis results of GSVA (Gene Set Variation Analysis) enrichment scores comparing CD vs NC groups. GSVA computes per-sample pathway activity scores independent of differential metabolite counts, offering complementary sensitivity. Columns: Pathway, Class, logFC (log2 fold-change of GSVA scores between groups), AveExpr (average expression across all samples), t_statistic (moderated t-statistic), pvalue (raw p-value), pvalue_adj (FDR-corrected p-value), significant (TRUE/FALSE at FDR <0.05), Direction (Up/Down/Not Significant). Three pathways were significantly upregulated: 'Cancer: overview' (logFC=+0.71, p_adj=7.2e-4), 'Glycan biosynthesis and metabolism' (logFC=+0.56, p_adj=9.7e-4), and 'Sensory system' (logFC=+0.61, p_adj=7.6e-3). These reflect host defense and stress signaling triggered by CDI infection itself."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/5_kegg_functional_analysis\\tables\\kegg_gsva_differential_FE_vs_CD.csv",
 "sheet_name": "gsva_diff_FE_vs_CD",
 "annotation": "LIMMA differential analysis of GSVA enrichment scores comparing FE (high-iron diet + CDI) vs CD (standard diet + CDI) groups — the core comparison. Columns: Pathway, Class, logFC, AveExpr, t_statistic, pvalue, pvalue_adj, significant, Direction.13 of22 pathways were significantly altered (FDR <0.05):6 upregulated,7 downregulated. Key findings: 'Signal transduction' (logFC=-0.96, p_adj=3.4e-6) was the most strongly downregulated pathway, suggesting high-iron diet suppresses host immune signaling during CDI (kb: 'high-iron alters neutrophil recruitment'). 'Energy metabolism' (logFC=+0.90, p_adj=8.6e-5) and 'Excretory system' (logFC=+0.73, p_adj=2.3e-5) were strongly upregulated, reflecting enhanced mitochondrial respiration and iron-detoxification mechanisms. 'Nervous system' (logFC=-0.77, p_adj=3.5e-5) was downregulated, indicating gut-brain axis perturbation. 'Lipid metabolism' was downregulated (logFC=-0.22, p_adj=0.039), directly supporting the hypothesis that high-iron diet reduces SCFA-producing beneficial bacteria."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/5_kegg_functional_analysis\\tables\\kegg_gsva_differential_FE_vs_NC.csv",
 "sheet_name": "gsva_diff_FE_vs_NC",
 "annotation": "LIMMA differential analysis of GSVA enrichment scores comparing FE (high-iron diet + CDI) vs NC (normal control) groups, reflecting the overall effect of the combined intervention. Columns: Pathway, Class, logFC, AveExpr, t_statistic, pvalue, pvalue_adj, significant, Direction.9 of22 pathways were significant (FDR <0.05), with a pattern highly similar to FE_vs_CD: 'Signal transduction' down (logFC=-0.70, p_adj=6.4e-5), 'Energy metabolism' up (logFC=+0.90, p_adj=6.4e-5), 'Excretory system' up (logFC=+0.87, p_adj=3.4e-6), 'Nervous system' down (logFC=-0.90, p_adj=5.4e-6), 'Lipid metabolism' down (logFC=-0.26, p_adj=0.020). This confirms that high-iron diet induces similar pathway activity alterations regardless of infection context, with consistent suppression of signal transduction and lipid metabolism, and activation of energy metabolism and excretory pathways."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/5_kegg_functional_analysis\\tables\\kegg_gsva_scores.csv",
 "sheet_name": "kegg_gsva_scores",
 "annotation": "GSVA enrichment scores for22 KEGG pathways across all18 samples. Columns: Pathway (KEGG pathway name), Fc16YH_CD1 through Fc16YH_CD6 (6 CD group samples: standard diet + CDI), Fc16YH_FE1 through Fc16YH_FE6 (6 FE group samples: high-iron diet + CDI), Fc16YH_NC1 through Fc16YH_NC6 (6 NC group samples: healthy controls). Positive scores indicate pathway activation; negative scores indicate suppression relative to the whole sample set. Samples cluster by group (NC→CD→FE) with clear separation: NC samples show high activity in 'Lipid metabolism' and 'Cofactor/vitamin metabolism', FE samples show high activity in 'Energy metabolism' and 'Excretory system', while CD samples exhibit intermediate profiles. This matrix can be used for hierarchical clustering, group comparisons, and correlation analyses with metabolite or microbiome features."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/5_kegg_functional_analysis\\tables\\kegg_pathway_background.csv",
 "sheet_name": "kegg_pathway_background",
 "annotation": "Background KEGG pathway mapping reference for all22 pathways used in this analysis. Columns: Pathway (KEGG pathway name and ID), Class (KEGG functional class hierarchy, e.g., '09100 Metabolism' for Metabolism, '09150 Organismal Systems'), N_Metabolites (number of detected metabolites successfully mapped to this pathway). The largest pathways are 'Biosynthesis of other secondary metabolites' (87 metabolites), 'Lipid metabolism' (73), and 'Amino acid metabolism' (56). Pathway class distribution: Metabolism (13 pathways,329 metabolites), Organismal Systems (5 pathways), Human Diseases (2), Environmental Information Processing (2), Cellular Processes (1), Genetic Information Processing (1). This background table defines the annotation scope used for both enrichment and GSVA analyses; note that only447 of2059 detected metabolites (21.7%) had KEGG pathway annotations."
 }
 ]
}

{
 "module_index":6,
 "module_name": "WGCNA Trait Association Analysis",
 "xlsx_file": "6_wgcna_trait_association_analysis.xlsx",
 "output_dir": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/analysis\\6_wgcna_trait_association_analysis",
 "sheets": [
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/6_wgcna_trait_association_analysis\\tables\\wgcna_all_traits.csv",
 "sheet_name": "wgcna_all_traits",
 "annotation": "This sheet contains the combined sample trait matrix used for WGCNA module-trait association analysis. Columns: 'Sample' (mouse sample ID), 'is_NC' (binary:1=NC healthy control group), 'is_CD' (binary:1=Clostridioides difficile infection group), 'is_FE' (binary:1=high-iron diet + CDI group), followed by26 KEGG pathway GSVA enrichment scores (e.g., '09103 Lipid metabolism', '09132 Signal transduction', '09155 Excretory system') providing multi-omics pathway-level phenotypes. Users can examine how each sample's group assignment and pathway activity profile correlate with module eigengenes to identify biologically relevant modules."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/6_wgcna_trait_association_analysis\\tables\\wgcna_gsva_scores.csv",
 "sheet_name": "wgcna_gsva_scores",
 "annotation": "This sheet contains the GSVA (Gene Set Variation Analysis) enrichment scores for26 KEGG pathways across all18 samples. Rows represent KEGG pathway categories (e.g., 'Signal transduction', 'Energy metabolism', 'Excretory system'), and columns represent individual samples across three groups: CD (C. difficile infection, Fc16YH_CD1-6), FE (high-iron diet + CDI, Fc16YH_FE1-6), and NC (healthy control, Fc16YH_NC1-6). These scores serve as quantitative pathway-level traits for module-trait correlation analysis, enabling the identification of WGCNA modules whose eigengenes significantly correlate with specific KEGG pathway activities."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/6_wgcna_trait_association_analysis\\tables\\wgcna_kegg_enrichment_all_modules.csv",
 "sheet_name": "wgcna_KEGG_enrichment",
 "annotation": "This sheet presents KEGG pathway enrichment results (Fisher's exact test) for metabolites in each WGCNA module. Columns: 'Module' (module color), 'Pathway' (KEGG pathway name), 'Count_in_module' (metabolites mapped to this pathway within the module), 'Pathway_size' (total metabolites in the pathway), 'Module_size' (total metabolites in the module), 'Bg_size' (background total metabolites, n=2059), 'Ratio' (Count_in_module/Module_size), 'pvalue' (Fisher's exact p-value), 'p_adj' (BH-adjusted p-value), 'enriched' (TRUE if p_adj<0.1). Key findings: Turquoise module (FE-up) shows enrichment in 'Biosynthesis of other secondary metabolites' (p=0.0049), consistent with high-iron diet promoting gut microbial secondary metabolism; Magenta module (CD-up) is significantly enriched in 'Cancer: overview' (p_adj=0.025), suggesting CDI-triggered cell stress and host defense signaling."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/6_wgcna_trait_association_analysis\\tables\\wgcna_module_colors.csv",
 "sheet_name": "wgcna_module_colors",
 "annotation": "This sheet records the module color assignment for each of the2,059 metabolites included in the WGCNA co-expression network. Columns: 'Feature' (metabolite ID), 'Module' (assigned module color: turquoise, blue, black, magenta, red, or purple). Six co-expression modules were identified via dynamic tree cutting (minClusterSize=10) and module merging (cutHeight=0.25) at soft-threshold power beta=6. Module sizes: turquoise (539,26.2%), black (577,28.0%), blue (511,24.8%), magenta (279,13.5%), red (116,5.6%), purple (37,1.8%). Users can use this sheet to retrieve which metabolites belong to which module for downstream analyses."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/6_wgcna_trait_association_analysis\\tables\\wgcna_module_eigengenes.csv",
 "sheet_name": "wgcna_module_eigengenes",
 "annotation": "This sheet contains the module eigengene (ME) values — the first principal component of each module's metabolite expression matrix — for all18 samples across the6 modules. Columns: 'Sample' (mouse sample ID), 'MEturquoise' through 'MEpurple' (module eigengene values). These MEs summarize the overall expression pattern of each co-expression module per sample and serve as the basis for module-trait correlation and regression analyses. Key results: Turquoise ME is strongly elevated in FE group (r=0.971 with is_FE, p=2.3e-11), while Magenta ME is selectively upregulated in CD group (r=0.892, p=6.8e-07), identifying these as hallmark modules for high-iron diet and C. difficile infection, respectively."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/6_wgcna_trait_association_analysis\\tables\\wgcna_module_membership.csv",
 "sheet_name": "wgcna_module_membership",
 "annotation": "This sheet quantifies the module membership (MM, also known as eigengene-based connectivity, kME) for each of the2,059 metabolites across all6 modules. Columns: 'Feature' (metabolite ID), 'Module' (assigned module), 'MM_turquoise' through 'MM_purple' (Pearson correlation between the metabolite's expression profile and each module eigengene). MM values range from -1 to1; metabolites with high absolute MM (e.g., |MM|>0.8) within their assigned module are considered hub metabolites. Users can identify the top hub metabolites (e.g., top30% by |MM|) per module for network visualization and to link specific metabolites to key biological modules associated with high-iron diet or CDI."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/6_wgcna_trait_association_analysis\\tables\\wgcna_module_trait_correlation.csv",
 "sheet_name": "wgcna_module_trait_corr",
 "annotation": "This sheet reports Pearson correlations between each module eigengene (ME) and each trait (3 group variables +26 KEGG pathway GSVA scores), forming the core module-trait association results. Columns: 'Module' (module color), 'Trait' (trait name), 'Correlation' (Pearson r), 'pvalue', 'Significance' (* p<0.05, ** p<0.01, *** p<0.001). Key findings: Turquoise module shows extremely strong positive correlation with FE group (r=0.971, p=2.3e-11) and strong negative correlation with Signal transduction pathway (r=-0.901, p=3.5e-07), supporting the hypothesis that high-iron diet suppresses host immune signaling. Red module is strongly positively correlated with Signal transduction (r=0.978, p=2.7e-12) and negatively with FE group (r=-0.913). Magenta module is strongly associated with CD group (r=0.892, p=6.8e-07), identifying it as the CDI-specific signature module."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/6_wgcna_trait_association_analysis\\tables\\wgcna_module_trait_regression.csv",
 "sheet_name": "wgcna_module_trait_reg",
 "annotation": "This sheet presents linear regression results (model: ME ~ is_FE + is_NC, with CD group as baseline) quantifying how group membership predicts module eigengene expression. Columns: 'Module' (module color), 'Term' (predictor: is_FE or is_NC), 'Estimate' (regression coefficient relative to CD baseline), 'StdError', 't_value', 'p_value', 'R_squared', 'Adj_R_squared', 'p_adj' (BH-corrected p-value). Key results: Turquoise module (R²=0.949, adj.R²=0.943) — is_FE highly positive (Estimate=+0.462, p=6.4e-10), confirming strong FE-specific activation. Red module (R²=0.861, adj.R²=0.842) — is_FE strongly negative (Estimate=-0.503, p=1.8e-07). Magenta module (R²=0.810, adj.R²=0.784) — both is_FE and is_NC negative, confirming CD-group-specific high expression."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/6_wgcna_trait_association_analysis\\tables\\wgcna_pathway_background.csv",
 "sheet_name": "wgcna_pathway_background",
 "annotation": "This sheet defines the KEGG pathway annotation background used for module enrichment analysis. Columns: 'Pathway' (KEGG pathway name), 'FeatureCount' (number of metabolites annotated to this pathway in the background set of2,059 metabolites), 'Features' (semicolon-separated list of metabolite IDs mapped to this pathway). This background represents the full set of KEGG-annotated metabolites from the metabolomics dataset, against which the enrichment of each module's metabolite set is tested using Fisher's exact test. Users can review pathway coverage and metabolite composition of the entire dataset."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/6_wgcna_trait_association_analysis\\tables\\wgcna_soft_threshold.csv",
 "sheet_name": "wgcna_soft_threshold",
 "annotation": "This sheet contains the scale-free topology fit indices for soft-thresholding power selection in WGCNA network construction. Columns: 'Power' (soft-threshold exponent beta, tested from1 to15), 'SFT_R_sq' (scale-free topology model fitting R²), 'Slope' (regression slope of log(k) vs log(p(k))), 'Mean_k' (mean connectivity), 'Median_k' (median connectivity), 'Max_k' (maximum connectivity). The optimal power beta=6 was selected (signed network), achieving SFT R²=0.63 with moderate mean connectivity (Mean_k=202), satisfying the scale-free network approximation. This threshold was used to construct the adjacency matrix and topological overlap matrix (TOM) for module detection."
 }
 ]
}

{
 "module_index":8,
 "module_name": "Dynamic Bayesian Network Analysis",
 "xlsx_file": "8_dynamic_bayesian_network_analysis.xlsx",
 "output_dir": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/analysis\\8_dynamic_bayesian_network_analysis",
 "sheets": [
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/8_dynamic_bayesian_network_analysis\\bayesian_module_eigengenes.csv",
 "sheet_name": "module_eigengenes_by_sample",
 "annotation": "This sheet contains the module eigengene (ME) expression values for each of the six WGCNA metabolic co-expression modules (turquoise, blue, red, magenta, black, purple) across eighteen samples from three groups (NC = healthy control, CD = C. difficile infection, FE = high-iron diet + C. difficile). Each row is a sample; the numeric columns represent the first principal component (eigengene) of each module, and the Group column indicates the experimental condition. The eigengene values serve as the input node features for the Bayesian network analysis, revealing a clear metabolic trajectory: NC samples exhibit high Blue (r = +0.919 with NC) and low Turquoise, CD samples show elevated Magenta (r = +0.892 with CD) and Red, while FE samples display strongly elevated Turquoise (r = +0.971 with FE) along with suppressed Blue and Red — directly supporting the high-iron diet-driven metabolic reprogramming hypothesis."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/8_dynamic_bayesian_network_analysis\\bayesian_network_arcs.csv",
 "sheet_name": "bayesian_network_arcs",
 "annotation": "This sheet lists the six high-confidence directed edges learned by the Hill-Climbing Bayesian network algorithm (BIC score,200 bootstrap replicates, confidence threshold >=0.7). Columns: 'from' = source module, 'to' = target module, 'strength' = arc confidence (0-1, proportion of bootstraps supporting this edge), 'direction' = directional confidence (0-1, fraction of supporting bootstraps agreeing on orientation). The top edge Turquoise -> Blue (strength =0.985) indicates that the high-iron diet signature module (Turquoise, FE group) exerts strong suppressive regulation on the healthy-baseline module (Blue, NC group). Turquoise -> Red (strength =0.870) directly supports the central hypothesis that high-iron diet suppresses host immune signaling (Red module, r = -0.913 with FE), consistent with knowledge-base mechanisms of iron-driven attenuation of neutrophil-mediated immunity. Magenta -> Blue and Magenta -> Red reflect C. difficile infection impacts on health and immunity, while Blue -> Red (strength =0.735) represents the positive support of healthy metabolism for immune signaling — a relationship disrupted after dietary intervention and infection."
 },
 {
 "csv": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/tmp/8_dynamic_bayesian_network_analysis\\bayesian_node_attributes.csv",
 "sheet_name": "bayesian_node_attributes",
 "annotation": "This sheet provides biological annotation for each of the six module nodes used in the Bayesian network. Columns: 'Node' = module color name, 'ModuleSize' = number of metabolites assigned to that module by WGCNA, 'AssociatedGroup' = the experimental group or biological process most strongly correlated with this module (with Pearson r value), 'CMeansCluster' = cluster assignment from the upstream CMeans clustering analysis (Cluster1-4), 'Color' = color hex code for visualization. Key biological mappings: Turquoise (539 metabolites, FE-associated, Cluster1) is the high-iron diet response module; Blue (511 metabolites, NC-associated, Cluster3) represents healthy baseline metabolism; Red (116 metabolites, signaling-associated, Cluster2) captures immune signal transduction; Magenta (279 metabolites, CD-associated, Cluster2) reflects C. difficile infection metabolic signature; Black (577 metabolites, Cluster4) represents basal/core metabolism; Purple (37 metabolites, Cluster4) is a minor module. This mapping bridges WGCNA co-expression modules with CMeans clustering (Fisher test p <0.0001) and assigns functional context to each network node."
 }
 ]
}

{
 "module_index":4,
 "module_name": "LIMMA Differential Analysis",
 "xlsx_file": "4_limma_differential_analysis.xlsx",
 "output_dir": "G:/OmicsWorks/test/metabolism/demo/analysis/4_limma_differential_analysis",
 "sheets": [
 {
